Q: Is this C macro for I/O error handling with a hidden return a reasonable practice? A: <p>Yes: <strong>the construct is standard C99+ and works as you intend</strong>, but I would probably change it—not because the one-shot <code>for</code> is invalid, but because it changes the meaning of surrounding control flow in ways that aren't obvious at the call site.</p>
<h3>1. Standard conformance</h3>
<p>This part is completely standard:</p>
<pre><code>for (int w = (cmd), _once = 1; _once; _once = 0)
</code></pre>
<p>C99 introduced declarations in the first clause of a <code>for</code>. The variables declared there have scope over the remainder of the declaration and the entire <code>for</code> statement, including its body. Thus <code>w</code> is correctly visible in your <code>else</code> statement.</p>
<p>In ordinary execution:</p>
<pre><code>w = cmd
_once = 1
test _once        -&gt; true
execute body
_once = 0
test _once        -&gt; false
</code></pre>
<p>So <code>cmd</code> is evaluated once, and there isn't a sequencing problem here. Initializers are full expressions and are separated appropriately for sequencing purposes.</p>
<p>Your expansion is essentially:</p>
<pre><code>int cnt = 0;

for (int w = fprintf(out, &quot;%s &quot;, msg), _once = 1;
     _once;
     _once = 0)
{
    if (w &lt; 0)
        return userraise(-1, ERR_STREAM_ERROR, &quot;IO error&quot;);
    else
        cnt += w;
}
</code></pre>
<p>So from a language-lawyer perspective, that part is sound.</p>
<p>There are, however, <strong>three important catches</strong>.</p>
<p>First, <code>break</code> and <code>continue</code> in the supplied statement don't do what a reader might expect:</p>
<pre><code>while (...) {
    IOCHECKER(w, foo(), -1) {
        if (something)
            break;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>That <code>break</code> breaks your <strong>hidden <code>for</code></strong>, not the visible <code>while</code>. C specifies that <code>break</code> applies to the smallest enclosing loop/switch.  Likewise, <code>continue</code> targets your hidden <code>for</code>; it executes <code>_once = 0</code> and terminates the macro loop rather than continuing an enclosing loop.</p>
<p>That's probably the biggest technical objection to this idiom.</p>
<p>Second, the syntax is unusual because:</p>
<pre><code>IOCHECKER(...)
    cnt += w;
</code></pre>
<p>must <strong>not</strong> become:</p>
<pre><code>IOCHECKER(...);
    cnt += w;
</code></pre>
<p>The latter supplies an empty statement as your <code>else</code> body. <code>w</code> then goes out of scope. Normally the compiler catches this, but if an outer <code>w</code> happens to exist it could produce much more confusing behaviour.</p>
<p>Third, <code>_once</code> isn't hygienic. It can shadow an identifier in the user's code:</p>
<pre><code>int _once = 123;

IOCHECKER(w, foo(), -1) {
    printf(&quot;%d&quot;, _once);   // this sees macro's _once, not 123
}
</code></pre>
<p>That's legal C, but undesirable macro behaviour.</p>
<h3>2. Hidden <code>return</code></h3>
<p>A hidden <code>return</code> isn't inherently terrible. I would consider something named explicitly like:</p>
<pre><code>RETURN_IF_IO_ERROR(...)
</code></pre>
<p>perfectly defensible in a C codebase.</p>
<p>The bigger concern is resource lifetime. Consider:</p>
<pre><code>FILE *a = fopen(...);
object *b = allocate_object();

IOCHECKER(w, fprintf(...), -1)
    foo(w);
</code></pre>
<p>If the macro returns, everything that needs manual cleanup has to have been accounted for. As functions become more complicated, this is where hidden returns tend to bite.</p>
<p>This is one reason conventional C often uses a common cleanup path. The Linux kernel coding guidelines, for example, explicitly recommend <code>goto</code> for functions with multiple failure paths requiring common cleanup.</p>
<p>So I'd distinguish two cases:</p>
<p><strong>Leaf/simple function with nothing to clean up:</strong> a return-on-error macro is quite reasonable.</p>
<p><strong>Function owning several resources:</strong> <code>goto cleanup</code> is usually cleaner.</p>
<h3>3. What I would use</h3>
<p>For your particular example, I'd sacrifice one additional line and make the macro a conventional statement:</p>
<pre><code>#define IO_CHECK_RETURN(result, ret)                         \
    do {                                                     \
        if ((result) &lt; 0)                                    \
            return userraise((ret), ERR_STREAM_ERROR,        \
                             &quot;IO error&quot;);                     \
    } while (0)
</code></pre>
<p>Then:</p>
<pre><code>int w = fprintf(out, &quot;%s &quot;, msg);
IO_CHECK_RETURN(w, -1);
cnt += w;
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>do { ... } while (0)</code> form is the usual way to make a multi-statement macro behave syntactically like one statement; GCC's own macro documentation discusses these kinds of macro pitfalls.</p>
<p>I'd actually put <code>RETURN</code> in the name specifically because of your concern about hidden control flow:</p>
<pre><code>IO_RETURN_IF_ERROR(w, -1);
</code></pre>
<p>Now a reviewer immediately knows that execution might leave the function there.</p>
<p>If cleanup is required, I'd instead do something like:</p>
<pre><code>int result = -1;
int w;

w = fprintf(out, &quot;%s &quot;, msg);
if (w &lt; 0) {
    userraise(-1, ERR_STREAM_ERROR, &quot;IO error&quot;);
    goto cleanup;
}

cnt += w;

/* more operations */

result = 0;

cleanup:
    fclose(out);
    free(buffer);
    return result;
</code></pre>
<p>Or wrap just the repetitive part:</p>
<pre><code>#define IO_CHECK_GOTO(x, label) \
    do {                         \
        if ((x) &lt; 0)             \
            goto label;          \
    } while (0)
</code></pre>
<p>Then:</p>
<pre><code>int w = fprintf(out, &quot;%s &quot;, msg);
IO_CHECK_GOTO(w, io_error);
cnt += w;
</code></pre>
<h3>One other issue with <code>IOCHECKER</code></h3>
<p>Your current macro says:</p>
<pre><code>int w = (cmd)
</code></pre>
<p>That makes it specifically suitable for functions returning <code>int</code>, such as <code>fprintf()</code>.</p>
<p>It should <strong>not really be a generic I/O macro</strong>. For example, POSIX <code>write()</code> returns <code>ssize_t</code>, while standard C <code>fread()</code> returns <code>size_t</code> and indicates short reads/errors differently. So both the return type and the test <code>&lt; 0</code> are properties of the particular API being wrapped.</p>
<p>That argues for either having narrowly named macros, or checking an already-declared result variable rather than having the macro declare its type.</p>
<h3>Bottom line</h3>
<p>I would rate your current approach approximately:</p>
<p><strong>Language correctness:</strong> 9/10 — fully legitimate standard-C technique.</p>
<p><strong>Readability:</strong> 7/10 once everyone knows the idiom.</p>
<p><strong>Robustness in arbitrary surrounding code:</strong> 5/10, primarily because <code>break</code>/<code>continue</code> bind to the invisible <code>for</code>.</p>
<p>The hidden <code>return</code> actually bothers me <strong>less than the hidden loop</strong>.</p>
<p>I'd therefore keep the basic idea but change it to:</p>
<pre><code>int w = fprintf(out, &quot;%s &quot;, msg);
IO_RETURN_IF_ERROR(w, -1);
cnt += w;
</code></pre>
<p>For a function that needs cleanup, I'd use <code>goto cleanup</code>. That combination is less clever, but it preserves almost all of the conciseness while making the actual C control structure visible to whoever reads the function.</p>

Q: How can I restrict a method to only classes implementing a specific interface in Java? A: <p>With a little changes...</p>
<pre><code>public class Healer extends GameCharacter implements Healable, Mensajera {

   private final int healingPower = 20;

   @Override
   public void heal( GameCharacter target ) {

        // we pass the object itself as a parameter
      target.heal( this );
   }

     // create a new method, to return the correct value 
   public int getAmount() {
      return healingPower;
   }
}

public class GameCharacter implements Mensajera {

   private String characterName;
   private int characterPower;
   private int characterMaximumHealth;
   private int characterCurrentHealth;

   // constructors, getters, etc.
   public void takeDamage( int damage ) {
      characterCurrentHealth = Math.max( 0, characterCurrentHealth - damage
      );
   }

   public void heal( Healer hel ) {

        // we obtain the correct value of the object that calls the method
      characterCurrentHealth = Math.min(
              characterMaximumHealth, characterCurrentHealth + hel.getAmount() );      
   }
}
</code></pre>

